\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{Limitations}
\date{2026-11-09}


\p{Current limitations are relative to the full goal in [[fdip-00AU]].}

\p{No physical environment map is constructed. \ref{fdip-003X} asks for
one. \ref{fdip-00G0} and \ref{fdip-00G1} give converging finite-range
families one normalized limiting law, at mass squared
#{\frac{\mu}{\kappa}}. \ref{fdip-00FY} with \ref{fdip-00FZ} select
exact representative shapes once their free inputs are fixed. These
comparisons leave the environment map, the physical values of those
inputs, a dynamical choice of symmetry or vacuum, and physical
spacetime unconstructed.}

\p{The backgrounds form a groupoid that carries no site, stack or descent. A
local-to-global statement about background objects would need a coverage on the
groupoid, sheaves of morphisms over it and effective descent, which
\ref{fdip-004Q} states with its source; \ref{fdip-0069} proves a finite
local-to-global theorem for laws and notes that it gives no descent data for the
objects and arrows of the background groupoid.}

\p{Some finite Gaussian families extend to a continuum Gaussian
field. For #{0<p\lambda<1} the hierarchical cell density is
square-integrable (\ref{fdip-00FG}). For #{p\lambda\ge 1} no
square-integrable point density matches every depth
(\ref{fdip-00FL}). The gasket graphs with edge weight
#{\bigl(\frac{5}{3}\bigr)^n} have a compact resistance
completion (\ref{fdip-00EN}), and \ref{fdip-00F5} states
its counting exponent.
These Gaussian scalar fields do not by themselves supply interacting
or fermionic dynamics.
The comparison on a supplied circle of \ref{fdip-004J} controls one
fixed Fourier mode, which is not a continuum limit of the full induced law.
\ref{fdip-003X} states what a physical interpretation would require, and
[Discrete induction and its open interfaces](fdip-003W) records which
comparisons remain open.}

\p{For polynomial interactions bounded below, \ref{fdip-00GI}
gives actual positive finite-dimensional laws and exact marginals.
The formal coefficients acquire the finite-order, compact-set bounds of
\ref{fdip-00GN}; they do not give a convergent infinite series or a
globally stable truncated density, as \ref{fdip-00GQ} explains.
The compressed vacuum evolution is a semigroup exactly when the retained
subspace is invariant under the full vacuum semigroup, by \ref{fdip-00HI}.}

\p{For bosonic scalar fields on finite spatial graphs, \ref{fdip-00GW} gives positive
interacting transfer and \ref{fdip-00GY} gives temporal reflection
positivity. The self-adjoint scalar Hamiltonian of \ref{fdip-00H2}
generates unitary real-time evolution by \ref{fdip-00HE}, with
finite-volume vacuum and thermal correlations in \ref{fdip-00HB}.
These statements require the declared coercive potential and time
conventions. They do not themselves give Lorentz covariance.}

\p{For the even quartic model with #{m_0>0} and
#{0<\lambda /m_0^2<\eta}, where \ref{fdip-00IC} specifies #{\eta},
\ref{fdip-00IH} constructs an interacting neutral scalar Wightman field in
#{1+1} dimensions. Its unitary representation of the proper
orthochronous Poincaré group, forward-cone spectrum, locality and unique
vacuum are continuum conclusions. The mesh is removed before the
spacetime volume grows, as in \ref{fdip-00I6}. This is a vacuum
construction; a continuum thermal state is not constructed here.
The result supplies neither an interacting spectral mass gap nor an
S-matrix, as \ref{fdip-00IK} explains.}

\p{For the finite massive graded Wilson class, the QED comparison uses a
positive #{U(1)} connection marginal (\ref{fdip-00IP}), exact Grassmann source
integration (\ref{fdip-00IV}), vector Ward identities (\ref{fdip-00J2}) and
dressed observations (\ref{fdip-00J5}). Connection positivity supplies neither a
fermionic Hilbert space nor electron dynamics; fermionic reconstruction
and continuum QED remain open here (\ref{fdip-00JB}). Maxwell scaling
(\ref{fdip-00J9}) concerns a classical action on smooth backgrounds.}

\p{No statement in these notes fixes a physical spacetime, a physical
parameter, a Standard Model equation or a gravitational equation.}
